1.Write a program that prints the numbers from 10 down to 1 using a for loop, similar to a countdown timer.
Ans.
#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
	int i;
	for(i=10;i>=1;i--)
	{
		printf("%d\n",i);
    }
}


2.Create a menu-driven console app that lets the user: 1) View your favorite 3 IPL teams, 2) Add a new team, 3) Exit. Use a while loop to keep showing the menu until the user chooses Exit.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Use input() (or Scanner in Java) to get the user's choice each time.</em>
Ans.
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    int choice;

    while(1) {
        printf("\n1. View IPL Teams");
        printf("\n2. Add New Team");
        printf("\n3. Exit");

        printf("\nEnter choice: ");
        scanf("%d",&choice);

        if(choice==1) {
            printf("1. Mumbai Indians");
            printf("\n2. Chennai Super Kings");
            printf("\n3. Rajasthan Royals\n");
        }
        else if(choice==2) {
            printf("new team added!\n");
        }
        else if(choice==3) {
            printf("exit");
            break;
        }
        else {
            printf("invalid choice!\n");
        }
    }
}


3.Build a 'Guess the Song' game like Spotify — the program randomly picks a song name from a list and asks the user to guess it. Use a do-while loop so the user can keep guessing until they get it right.<br><br><em><strong>Constraint:</strong> Use at least 3 song names of your choice.</em>
Ans.
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    int song,guess;
    song = 2;

    printf("Guess the Song!\n");
    printf("1. Kesariya\n");
    printf("2. Apna Bana Le\n");
    printf("3. Tum Hi Ho\n");

    do {
        printf("enter your guess:");
        scanf("%d",&guess);

        if(guess==song)
            printf("Correct! You guessed the song!");
        else
            printf("Wrong guess! Try again.\n");

    } while(guess!=song);
}


4.Explain with your own example the difference between entry-controlled and exit-controlled loops by writing a short code snippet for each (for/while vs do-while) and describing what happens if the loop condition is false at the start.
Ans.
-->Entry-controlled

#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    int i = 10;

    while(i < 5) {
        printf("%d", i);
        i++;
    }
}
The condition i < 5 is false at the beginning, so the loop runs 0 times.


-->Exit-controlled loop
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    int i = 10;

    do {
        printf("%d", i);
        i++;
    } while(i < 5);

    return 0;
}
The condition is false, but the do-while loop runs at least once.
